main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ move-constructed member holds moved value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
is not accessible
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [class.copy.ctor]/14-15: a defaulted move constructor direct-initializes
each base and non-static data member from the corresponding subobject of the
argument cast to an xvalue (static_cast<M&&>(other.m)); overload resolution on
that xvalue selects the member's move constructor.  A member whose copy
constructor is deleted but whose move constructor is usable must therefore be
moved, not copied.

CBMC's defaulted copy/move constructor synthesis (cpp_typecheck_function.cpp,
default_cpctor in cpp_typecheck_constructor.cpp) generates a memberwise *copy*
initializer `m(ref.m)` for the move constructor too, and its trivial-member
gate (is_trivial_copy) inspects only the member's data-member types -- so a
class like M, whose only data member is an int but whose copy constructor is
deleted, is treated as trivially copyable and copied.  The copy selects M's
=delete'd copy constructor ("member 'M::M(this, ...M&...)' is not accessible")
and the enclosing constructor body is dropped with a CONVERSION ERROR.  g++ and
clang++ accept this program.

This is the header-free, template-free root cause of the enable_if_t<false>
dog-food failures in parse_options.cpp, irep_serialization.cpp and
ui_message.cpp: all hold a std::unique_ptr member, whose copy constructor is
deleted and move constructor is user-provided; the defaulted move constructor
of the holder copies (rather than moves) the unique_ptr, and considering its
=delete'd deleter constructor overload additionally instantiates
enable_if_t<false>.

Flip to CORE once defaulted move/copy constructor synthesis move/copy-constructs
non-trivially-copyable members via the member's own move/copy constructor with
the correct value category.
